📊 Method Overloading vs Method Overloading

| Aspect                 | Method Overloading(Compile-time polymorphism)    | Method Overriding(Runtime polymorphism)                        |
|------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|
| Definition             | Defining multiple methods with the same name     | Defining a method in the derived class with the same name and  |
                           but different parameters/return types              parameters as in the base class
| Polymorphism Type      | Compile-time polymorphism                        | Runtime polymorphism                                           |
| Inheritance            | Not required                                     | Required                                                       |
| Parameters             | Must be different                                | Must be the same                                               |
| Return Type            | Can be different                                 | Usually the same (or covariant)                                |


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
📊 Abstract Class vs Interface

| Feature                   | Abstract Class                                              | Interface                                      |
| --------------------------| ----------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| 1. Multiple Inheritance   | Does not support multiple inheritance                       | Supports multiple inheritance                  |
| 2. Members & Constructors | Can contain data members and constructors                   | Does not contain constructors                  |
| 3. Methods                | Can have abstract and non-abstract methods                  | Can have abstract, default, and static methods |
| 4. Variables              | Can have final, non-final, static, and non-static variables | Only static and final variables                |
| 5. Access Modifiers       | Can have private, protected, public, and default members    | Members are public by default                  |
| 6. Example                | `abstract class Shape { abstract fun draw() }`              | `interface Shape { fun draw() }`               |
